2,000 gp}\cell\row\pard\trowd\trql\trleft-108\ltrrow\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx2419\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx3636\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx5414\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Serpent venom}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Injury}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
200 gp}\cell\row\pard\trowd\trql\trleft-108\ltrrow\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx2419\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx3636\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx5414\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Torpor}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Ingested}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
600 gp}\cell\row\pard\trowd\trql\trleft-108\ltrrow\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx2419\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx3636\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx5414\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Truth serum}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Ingested}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
150 gp}\cell\row\pard\trowd\trql\trleft-108\ltrrow\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx2419\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx3636\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx5414\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Wyvern poison}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
Injury}\cell\pard\plain \s68\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb36\sa36\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0\intbl\ql\nowidctlpar\sb36\sa36{\rtlch \ltrch\i\loch
1,200 gp}\cell\row\pard\pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Sample Poisons}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Each type of poison has its own debilitating effects.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Assassin\u8217\'92s Blood (Ingested).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must make a DC 10 Constitution saving throw. On a failed save, it takes 6 (1d12) poison damage and is poisoned for 24 hours. On a successful save, the creature takes half damage and isn\u8217\'92t poisoned.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Burnt Othur Fumes (Inhaled).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 13 Constitution saving throw or take 10 (3d6) poison damage, and must repeat the saving throw at the start of each of its turns. On each successive failed save, the character takes 3 (1d6) poison damage. After three successful saves, the poison ends.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Crawler Mucus (Contact).}{\loch
 This poison must be harvested from a dead or }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#incapacitated" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
incapacitated}{}}}\loch
 crawler. A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 13 Constitution saving throw or be poisoned for 1 minute. The poisoned creature is }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#paralyzed" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
paralyzed}{}}}\loch
. The creature can repeat the saving throw at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on itself on a success.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Drow Poison (Injury).}{\loch
 This poison is typically made only by the drow, and only in a place far removed from sunlight. A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 13 Constitution saving throw or be poisoned for 1 hour. If the saving throw fails by 5 or more, the creature is also }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#unconscious" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
unconscious}{}}}\loch
 while poisoned in this way. The creature wakes up if it takes damage or if another creature takes an action to shake it awake.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Essence of Ether (Inhaled).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 8 hours. The poisoned creature is }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#unconscious" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
unconscious}{}}}\loch
. The creature wakes up if it takes damage or if another creature takes an action to shake it awake.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Malice (Inhaled).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 1 hour. The poisoned creature is }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#blinded" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
blinded}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Midnight Tears (Ingested).}{\loch
 A creature that ingests this poison suffers no effect until the stroke of midnight. If the poison has not been neutralized before then, the creature must succeed on a DC 17 Constitution saving throw, taking 31 (9d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Oil of Taggit (Contact).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 13 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 24 hours. The poisoned creature is }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#unconscious" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
unconscious}{}}}\loch
. The creature wakes up if it takes damage.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Pale Tincture (Ingested).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 16 Constitution saving throw or take 3 (1d6) poison damage and become poisoned. The poisoned creature must repeat the saving throw every 24 hours, taking 3 (1d6) poison damage on a failed save. Until this poison ends, the damage the poison deals can\u8217\'92t be healed by any means. After seven successful saving throws, the effect ends and the creature can heal normally.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Purple Worm Poison (Injury).}{\loch
 This poison must be harvested from a dead or }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#incapacitated" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
incapacitated}{}}}\loch
 purple worm. A creature subjected to this poison must make a DC 19 Constitution saving throw, taking 42 (12d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Serpent Venom (Injury).}{\loch
 This poison must be harvested from a dead or }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#incapacitated" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
incapacitated}{}}}\loch
 giant poisonous snake. A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution saving throw, taking 10 (3d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Torpor (Ingested).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 4d6 hours. The poisoned creature is }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#incapacitated" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
incapacitated}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Truth Serum (Ingested).}{\loch
 A creature subjected to this poison must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution saving throw or become poisoned for 1 hour. The poisoned creature can\u8217\'92t knowingly speak a lie, as if under the effect of a }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#zone-of-truth" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\i\loch
zone of truth}{}}}\loch
 spell.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Wyvern Poison (Injury).}{\loch
 This poison must be harvested from a dead or }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#incapacitated" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
incapacitated}{}}}\loch{\*\bkmkstart section-poisons}{\*\bkmkstart poisons}{\*\bkmkend section-poisons}{\*\bkmkend poisons}
 wyvern. A creature subjected to this poison must make a DC 15 Constitution saving throw, taking 24 (7d6) poison damage on a failed save, or half as much damage on a successful one.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs28\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs36\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Diseases}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A plague ravages the kingdom, setting the adventurers on a quest to find a cure. An adventurer emerges from an ancient tomb, unopened for centuries, and soon finds herself suffering from a wasting illness. A warlock offends some dark power and contracts a strange affliction that spreads whenever he casts spells.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A simple outbreak might amount to little more than a small drain on party resources, curable by a casting of }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#lesser-restoration" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\i\loch
lesser restoration}{}}}\loch
. A more complicated outbreak can form the basis of one or more adventures as characters search for a cure, stop the spread of the disease, and deal with the consequences.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A disease that does more than infect a few party members is primarily a plot device. The rules help describe the effects of the disease and how it can be cured, but the specifics of how a disease works aren\u8217\'92t bound by a common set of rules. Diseases can affect any creature, and a given illness might or might not pass from one race or kind of creature to another. A plague might affect only constructs or undead, or sweep through a halfling neighborhood but leave other races untouched. What matters is the story you want to tell.}
\par \pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Sample Diseases}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
The diseases here illustrate the variety of ways disease can work in the game. Feel free to alter the saving throw DCs, incubation times, symptoms, and other characteristics of these diseases to suit your campaign.}
\par \pard\plain \s4\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs24\lang1033\i0\b\kerning0{\loch
Cackle Fever}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
This disease targets humanoids, although }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-gnome" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
gnomes}{}}}\loch
 are strangely immune. While in the grips of this disease, victims frequently succumb to fits of mad laughter, giving the disease its common name and its morbid nickname: \u8220\'93the shrieks.\u8221\'94}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Symptoms manifest 1d4 hours after infection and include fever and disorientation. The infected creature gains one level of }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#exhaustion" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
exhaustion}{}}}\loch
 that can\u8217\'92t be removed until the disease is cured.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Any event that causes the infected creature great stress\u8212\'97including entering combat, taking damage, experiencing fear, or having a nightmare\u8212\'97forces the creature to make a DC 13 Constitution saving throw. On a failed save, the creature takes 5 (1d10) psychic damage and becomes }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#incapacitated" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
incapacitated}{}}}\loch
 with mad laughter for 1 minute. The creature can repeat the saving throw at the end of each of its turns, ending the mad laughter and the incapacitated condition on a success.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Any humanoid creature that starts its turn within 10 feet of an infected creature in the throes of mad laughter must succeed on a DC 10 Constitution saving throw or also become infected with the disease. Once a creature succeeds on this save, it is immune to the mad laughter of that particular infected creature for 24 hours.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
At the end of each long rest, an infected creature can make a DC 13 Constitution saving throw. On a successful save, the DC for this save and for the save to avoid an attack of mad laughter drops by 1d6. When the saving throw DC drops to 0, the creature recovers from the disease. A creature that fails three of these saving throws gains a randomly determined form of indefinite }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-madness" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
madness}{}}}\loch{\*\bkmkstart cackle-fever}{\*\bkmkend cackle-fever}
.}
\par \pard\plain \s4\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs24\lang1033\i0\b\kerning0{\loch
Sewer Plague}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Sewer plague is a generic term for a broad category of illnesses that incubate in sewers, refuse heaps, and stagnant swamps, and which are sometimes transmitted by creatures that dwell in those areas, such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#rat" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
rats}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#otyugh" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
otyughs}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
When a humanoid creature is bitten by a creature that carries the disease, or when it comes into contact with filth or offal contaminated by the disease, the creature must succeed on a DC 11 Constitution saving throw or become infected.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
It takes 1d4 days for sewer plague\u8217\'92s symptoms to manifest in an infected creature. Symptoms include fatigue and cramps. The infected creature suffers one level of }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#exhaustion" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
exhaustion}{}}}\loch
, and it regains only half the normal number of hit points from spending Hit Dice and no hit points from finishing a long rest.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch{\*\bkmkstart sewer-plague}{\*\bkmkend sewer-plague}
At the end of each long rest, an infected creature must make a DC 11 Constitution saving throw. On a failed save, the character gains one level of exhaustion. On a successful save, the character\u8217\'92s exhaustion level decreases by one level. If a successful saving throw reduces the infected creature\u8217\'92s level of exhaustion below 1, the creature recovers from the disease.}
\par \pard\plain \s4\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs24\lang1033\i0\b\kerning0{\loch
Sight Rot}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
This painful infection causes bleeding from the eyes and eventually blinds the victim.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A beast or humanoid that drinks water tainted by sight rot must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution saving throw or become infected. One day after infection, the creature\u8217\'92s vision starts to become blurry. The creature takes a \u8722\'3f1 penalty to attack rolls and ability checks that rely on sight. At the end of each long rest after the symptoms appear, the penalty worsens by 1. When it reaches \u8722\'3f5, the victim is }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#blinded" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
blinded}{}}}\loch
 until its sight is restored by magic such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#lesser-restoration" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\i\loch
lesser restoration}{}}}\loch
 or }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#heal" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\i\loch
heal}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch{\*\bkmkstart section-diseases}{\*\bkmkstart diseases}{\*\bkmkstart sample-diseases}{\*\bkmkstart sight-rot}{\*\bkmkend section-diseases}{\*\bkmkend diseases}{\*\bkmkend sample-diseases}{\*\bkmkend sight-rot}
Sight rot can be cured using a rare flower called Eyebright, which grows in some swamps. Given an hour, a character who has proficiency with an herbalism kit can turn the flower into one dose of ointment. Applied to the eyes before a long rest, one dose of it prevents the disease from worsening after that rest. After three doses, the ointment cures the disease entirely.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs28\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs36\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Madness}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
In a typical campaign, characters aren\u8217\'92t driven mad by the horrors they face and the carnage they inflict day after day, but sometimes the stress of being an adventurer can be too much to bear. If your campaign has a strong horror theme, you might want to use madness as a way to reinforce that theme, emphasizing the extraordinarily horrific nature of the threats the adventurers face.}
\par \pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Going Mad}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Various magical effects can inflict madness on an otherwise stable mind. Certain spells, such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#contact-other-plane" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\i\loch
contact other plane}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#symbol" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\i\loch
symbol}{}}}\loch
, can cause insanity, and you can use the madness rules here instead of the spell effects of those spells. Diseases, poisons, and planar effects such as psychic wind or the howling winds of a chaotic plane can all inflict madness. Some artifacts can also break the psyche of a character who uses or becomes }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-attunement" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
attuned}{}}}\loch
 to them.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch{\*\bkmkstart going-mad}{\*\bkmkend going-mad}
Resisting a madness-inducing effect usually requires a Wisdom or Charisma saving throw.}
\par \pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Madness Effects}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Madness can be short-term, long-term, or indefinite. Most relatively mundane effects impose short-term madness, which lasts for just a few minutes. More horrific effects or cumulative effects can result in long-term or indefinite madness.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A character afflicted with }{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
short-term madness}{\loch
