Familiarity.}{\loch
 \u8220\'93Permanent circle\u8221\'94 means a permanent teleportation circle whose sigil sequence you know. \u8220\'93Associated object\u8221\'94 means that you possess an object taken from the desired destination within the last six months, such as a book from a wizard\u8217\'92s library, bed linen from a royal suite, or a chunk of marble from a lich\u8217\'92s secret tomb.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
Very familiar\u8221\'94 is a place you have been very often, a place you have carefully studied, or a place you can see when you cast the spell. \u8220\'93Seen casually\u8221\'94 is someplace you have seen more than once but with which you aren\u8217\'92t very familiar. \u8220\'93Viewed once\u8221\'94 is a place you have seen once, possibly using magic. \u8220\'93Description\u8221\'94 is a place whose location and appearance you know through someone else\u8217\'92s description, perhaps from a map.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{
\u8220\'93}{\loch
False destination\u8221\'94 is a place that doesn\u8217\'92t exist. Perhaps you tried to scry an enemy\u8217\'92s sanctum but instead viewed an illusion, or you are attempting to teleport to a familiar location that no longer exists.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
On Target.}{\loch
 You and your group (or the target object) appear where you want to.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Off Target.}{\loch
 You and your group (or the target object) appear a random distance away from the destination in a random direction. Distance off target is 1d10 \u215\'d7 1d10 percent of the distance that was to be traveled. For example, if you tried to travel 120 miles, landed off target, and rolled a 5 and 3 on the two d10s, then you would be off target by 15 percent, or 18 miles. The GM determines the direction off target randomly by rolling a d8 and designating 1 as north, 2 as northeast, 3 as east, and so on around the points of the compass. If you were teleporting to a coastal city and wound up 18 miles out at sea, you could be in trouble.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Similar Area.}{\loch
 You and your group (or the target object) wind up in a different area that\u8217\'92s visually or thematically similar to the target area. If you are heading for your home laboratory, for example, you might wind up in another wizard\u8217\'92s laboratory or in an alchemical supply shop that has many of the same tools and implements as your laboratory. Generally, you appear in the closest similar place, but since the spell has no range limit, you could conceivably wind up anywhere on the plane.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ai\ab \ltrch\i\b\loch
Mishap.}{\loch{\*\bkmkstart teleport}{\*\bkmkend teleport}
 The spell\u8217\'92s unpredictable magic results in a difficult journey. Each teleporting creature (or the target object) takes 3d10 force damage, and the GM rerolls on the table to see where you wind up (multiple mishaps can occur, dealing damage each time).}
