Modifying Creatures}
\par \pard\plain \s75\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\li480\ri480\lin480\rin480\fi0\sb100\sa100\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch{\*\bkmkstart size-1}{\*\bkmkend size-1}
You might be at a loss when it comes to finding the perfect creature for part of an adventure. Feel free to tweak an existing creature to make it into something more useful for you, perhaps by borrowing a trait or two from a different monster or by using a variant or template, such as the ones in this book. Keep in mind that modifying a monster, including when you apply a template to it, might change its challenge rating.}
\par \pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Type}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A monster\u8217\'92s type speaks to its fundamental nature. Certain spells, magic items, class features, and other effects in the game interact in special ways with creatures of a particular type. For example, an }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\i\loch
arrow of dragon slaying}{\loch
 deals extra damage not only to dragons but also other creatures of the dragon type, such as dragon turtles and wyverns.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
The game includes the following monster types, which have no rules of their own.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Aberrations}{\loch
 are utterly alien beings. Many of them have innate magical abilities drawn from the creature\u8217\'92s alien mind rather than the mystical forces of the world. The quintessential aberrations are }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#aboleth" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
aboleths}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Beasts}{\loch
 are nonhumanoid creatures that are a natural part of the fantasy ecology. Some of them have magical powers, but most are unintelligent and lack any society or language. Beasts include all varieties of ordinary }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#chapter-miscellaneous-creatures" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
animals}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#dinosaurs" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
dinosaurs}{}}}\loch
, and giant versions of animals.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Celestials}{\loch
 are creatures native to the upper planes. Many of them are the servants of deities, employed as messengers or agents in the mortal realm and throughout the planes. Celestials are good by nature, so the exceptional celestial who strays from a good alignment is a horrifying rarity. Celestials include }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#angels" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
angels}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#couatl" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
couatls}{}}}\loch
, and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#pegasus" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
pegasi}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Constructs}{\loch
 are made, not born. Some are programmed by their creators to follow a simple set of instructions, while others are imbued with sentience and capable of independent thought. }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#golems" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
Golems}{}}}\loch
 are the iconic constructs. Many creatures native to certain outer planes are constructs shaped from the raw material of the plane by the will of more powerful creatures.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Dragons}{\loch
 are large reptilian creatures of ancient origin and tremendous power. True dragons, including the good }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#dragons-metallic" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
metallic dragons}{}}}\loch
 and the evil }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#dragons-chromatic" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
chromatic dragons}{}}}\loch
, are highly intelligent and have innate magic. Also in this category are creatures distantly related to true dragons, but less powerful, less intelligent, and less magical, such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#wyvern" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
wyverns}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#pseudodragon" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
pseudodragons}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Elementals}{\loch
 are creatures native to the elemental planes. Some creatures of this type are little more than animate masses of their respective elements, including the creatures simply called elementals. Others have biological forms infused with elemental energy. The races of }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#genies" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
genies}{}}}\loch
, including }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#djinni" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
djinn}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#efreeti" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
efreet}{}}}\loch
, form the most important civilizations on the elemental planes. Other elemental creatures include }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#azer" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
azers}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#invisible-stalker" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
invisible stalkers}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Fey}{\loch
 are magical creatures closely tied to the forces of nature. They dwell in twilight groves and misty forests. In some worlds, they are closely tied to the fey plane. Some are also found in the outer planes, particularly nature-oriented planes. Fey include }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#dryad" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
dryads}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#satyr" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
satyrs}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Fiends}{\loch
 are creatures of wickedness that are native to the lower planes. A few are the servants of deities, but many more labor under the leadership of archdevils and demon princes. Evil priests and mages sometimes summon fiends to the material world to do their bidding. If an evil celestial is a rarity, a good fiend is almost inconceivable. Fiends include }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#demons" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
demons}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#devils" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
devils}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#hell-hound" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
hell hounds}{}}}\loch
, and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#rakshasa" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
rakshasas}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Giants}{\loch
 tower over humans and their kind. They are humanlike in shape, though some have multiple heads (}{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#ettin" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
ettins}{}}}\loch
) or deformities. The six varieties of true giant are }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#hill-giant" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
hill giants}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#stone-giant" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
stone giants}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#frost-giant" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
frost giants}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#fire-giant" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
fire giants}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#cloud-giant" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
cloud giants}{}}}\loch
, and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#storm-giant" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
storm giants}{}}}\loch
. Besides these, creatures such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#ogre" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
ogres}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#troll" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
trolls}{}}}\loch
 are giants.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Humanoids}{\loch
 are the main peoples of a fantasy gaming world, both civilized and savage, including humans and a tremendous variety of other species. They have language and culture, few if any innate magical abilities (though most humanoids can learn spellcasting), and a bipedal form. The most common humanoid races are the ones most suitable as player characters: }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-human" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
humans}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-dwarf" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
dwarves}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-elf" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
elves}{}}}\loch
, and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-halfling" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
halflings}{}}}\loch
. Almost as numerous but far more savage and brutal, and almost uniformly evil, are the races of goblinoids (}{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#goblin" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
goblins}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#hobgoblin" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
hobgoblins}{}}}\loch
, and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#bugbear" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
bugbears}{}}}\loch
), }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#orc" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
orcs}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#gnoll" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
gnolls}{}}}\loch
, }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#lizardfolk" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
lizardfolk}{}}}\loch
, and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#kobold" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
kobolds}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Monstrosities}{\loch
 are monsters in the strictest sense\u8212\'97frightening creatures that are not ordinary, not truly natural, and almost never benign. Some are the results of magical experimentation gone awry (such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#owlbear" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
owlbears}{}}}\loch
), and others are the product of terrible curses (including }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#minotaur" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
minotaurs}{}}}\loch
). They defy categorization, and in some sense serve as a catch-all category for creatures that don\u8217\'92t fit into any other type.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Oozes}{\loch
 are gelatinous creatures that rarely have a fixed shape. They are mostly subterranean, dwelling in caves and dungeons and feeding on refuse, carrion, or creatures unlucky enough to get in their way. }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#black-pudding" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
Black puddings}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#gelatinous-cube" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
gelatinous cubes}{}}}\loch
 are among the most recognizable oozes.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Plants}{\loch
 in this context are vegetable creatures, not ordinary flora. Most of them are ambulatory, and some are carnivorous. The quintessential plants are the }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#shambling-mound" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
shambling mound}{}}}\loch
 and the treant. }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#fungi" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
Fungal creatures}{}}}\loch
, such as the }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#shrieker" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
shrieker}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#violet-fungus" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
violet fungus}{}}}\loch
, also fall into this category.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
Undead}{\loch
 are once-living creatures brought to a horrifying state of undeath through the practice of necromantic magic or some unholy curse. Undead include walking corpses, such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#vampires" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
vampires}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#zombies" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
zombies}{}}}\loch
, as well as bodiless spirits, such as }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#ghost" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
ghosts}{}}}\loch
 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#specter" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
specters}{}}}\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s4\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs24\lang1033\i0\b\kerning0{\loch
Tags}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch{\*\bkmkstart tags}{\*\bkmkend tags}
A monster might have one or more tags appended to its type, in parentheses. For example, an orc has the humanoid (orc) type. The parenthetical tags provide additional categorization for certain creatures. The tags have no rules of their own, but something in the game, such as a magic item, might refer to them. For instance, a spear that is especially effective at fighting demons would work against any monster that has the demon tag.}
\par \pard\plain \s4\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs24\lang1033\i0\b\kerning0{\loch
Alignment}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A monster\u8217\'92s alignment provides a clue to its disposition and how it behaves in a roleplaying or combat situation. For example, a chaotic evil monster might be difficult to reason with and might attack characters on sight, whereas a neutral monster might be willing to negotiate. See \u8220\'93}{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-alignment" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
Alignment}{}}}\loch
\u8221\'94 for descriptions of the different alignments.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
The alignment specified in a monster\u8217\'92s stat block is the default. Feel free to depart from it and change a monster\u8217\'92s alignment to suit the needs of your campaign. If you want a good-aligned green dragon or an evil storm giant, there\u8217\'92s nothing stopping you.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Some creatures can have }{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
any alignment}{\loch
. In other words, you choose the monster\u8217\'92s alignment. Some monsters\u8217\'92 alignment entries indicate a tendency or aversion toward law, chaos, good, or evil. For example, a }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#berserker" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
berserker}{}}}\loch
 can be any chaotic alignment (chaotic good, chaotic neutral, or chaotic evil), as befits its wild nature.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
Many creatures of low intelligence have no comprehension of law or chaos, good or evil. They don\u8217\'92t make moral or ethical choices, but rather act on instinct. These creatures are }{\rtlch\ab \ltrch\b\loch
unaligned}{\loch{\*\bkmkstart type}{\*\bkmkstart alignment-2}{\*\bkmkstart alignment-3}{\*\bkmkend type}{\*\bkmkend alignment-2}{\*\bkmkend alignment-3}
, which means they don\u8217\'92t have an alignment.}
\par \pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Armor Class}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch{\*\bkmkstart armor-class-1}{\*\bkmkend armor-class-1}
A monster that wears armor or carries a shield has an Armor Class (AC) that takes its armor, shield, and Dexterity into account. Otherwise, a monster\u8217\'92s AC is based on its Dexterity modifier and natural armor, if any. If a monster has natural armor, wears armor, or carries a shield, this is noted in parentheses after its AC value.}
\par \pard\plain \s3\dbch\af9\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ab\ql\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb200\sa0\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brdrcf31\brsp20\keepn\ltrpar\cf17\loch\f4\fs32\lang1033\b\kerning0{\loch
Hit Points}
\par \pard\plain \s67\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A monster usually dies or is destroyed when it drops to 0 hit points. For more on hit points, see \u8220\'93}{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#section-damage-and-healing" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ltrch\hich\af4\cf17\loch\f4\fs22\loch
Damage and Healing}{}}}\loch
\u8221\'94.}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A monster\u8217\'92s hit points are presented both as a die expression and as an average number. For example, a monster with 2d8 hit points has 9 hit points on average (2 \u215\'d7 4 1/2).}
\par \pard\plain \s63\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb180\sa180\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f4\fs22\lang1033\kerning0{\loch
A monster\u8217\'92s size determines the die used to calculate its hit points, as shown in the Hit Dice by Size table.}
\par \pard\plain \s79\dbch\af10\langfe1033\dbch\af9\afs24\alang1025\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa120\keepn\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f7\fs24\lang1033\i0\b\kerning0{\loch
Hit Dice by Size}
